.TH CHECKIN-BASH-GUARD 1 "2026-11-09" "cti-agent 1.6" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
checkin-bash-guard \- refuse shell syntax on the orchestrator heartbeat's Bash calls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B checkin\-bash\-guard
.PP
Not run by hand: Claude Code starts it as a
.B PreToolUse
hook and writes one JSON event to its standard input.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B checkin\-bash\-guard
is a Claude Code hook, configured for the tool
.B Bash
in the root-owned
.I $FLEET_CODE/checkin\-settings.json
and installed root-owned in
.IR $FLEET_CODE/bin ,
where
.BR run\-checkin (1)
checks it with the rest of the code. It is a Python 3 script run with
.BR "python3 \-I" ,
so nothing in the working directory, the environment or the service account's
site-packages can load code into it.
.PP
The heartbeat's tool grant is a list of prefix rules such as
.BR "$FLEET_CODE/bin/fleet\-timers:*" .
A rule names the command, not the shell around it. Had one ever matched
.B fleet\-timers > state/budget.json
every grant would be an arbitrary file write as the service account, and
.B cmd $(other)
would run something that was never granted. Whether Claude Code's own matcher
refuses each of those is its business; this hook makes the answer not depend
on it. A beat's command is therefore one granted command with plain arguments.
.PP
The hook blocks any
.B Bash
command that contains one of these characters anywhere, quoted or not:
.PP
.EX
<  >  |  ;  &  ` (backtick)  newline  carriage return
.EE
.PP
Outside single quotes it also blocks the characters bash expands:
.PP
.EX
$  *  ?  [  ~
.EE
.PP
.B $
is blocked in every form, plain
.B $NAME
included, because the beat's environment carries the
.I fleet.env
secrets: a
.B $CLIENT_SECRET
could otherwise reach the board or a
.BR cti\-alert
mail. A command that starts with a
.IB NAME = value
prefix is blocked too, since that changes what a granted command reads.
Single-quoted text is literal to bash and is not checked for the second set, so a
pattern such as
.B 'cti\-agent\-*'
stays usable. The hook finds those spans by reading quotes the way bash
does: a
.B '
inside double quotes is an ordinary character, and double-quoted text is
checked like unquoted text, since bash expands
.B $
there. A backslash outside single quotes and an unbalanced quote are blocked,
because either one moves where bash thinks a quote ends. The beat types absolute paths and never
.BR $FLEET_CODE .
Other tools pass untouched. Blocking is exit status 2 with the reason on
standard error, which Claude Code returns to the model so that it can reword
the call.
.PP
The hook fails closed. Input that is not a JSON object, a
.B Bash
event with no command string, or any error in the hook itself also exits 2. A
guard that fails open is not a guard.
.PP
Two more checks follow the syntax rules (PrimeSec design review 39260,
Scenario\-223 and Scenario\-224). The
.B argument policy
is a static table in the guard of what each granted command may take:
subcommands, flags, values and lengths (a board message at most 400
characters, memory text 1500, an alert reason 800, printable ASCII only;
.B cti\-alert
only with
.B \-\-unit cti\-agent\-checkin.service
.BR "\-\-kind ESCALATION" ,
and exactly one of
.B \-\-for\-real
or
.BR \-\-dry\-run ).
A command not in the table, an unknown flag or an out\-of\-rule value is
refused. A test fails if
.BR run\-checkin 's
grant and the table differ.
.PP
The
.B per\-beat limits
count the beat's Bash calls under
.IR state/checkin\-counts/ID.json ,
a directory the beat cannot read, where ID is
.BR CHECKIN_BEAT_ID .
A call past
.B CHECKIN_MAX_BASH
(default 20),
.B CHECKIN_MAX_ALERTS
(2),
.B CHECKIN_MAX_BOARD_WRITES
(4) or
.B CHECKIN_MAX_DB_WRITES
(6) is refused with a message saying it is a safety limit, not an outage, and
logged to the journal under the tag
.BR checkin\-bash\-guard .
With no beat id (a hand run, a test) nothing is counted. A counter that
cannot be read or written refuses the call.
.SH OPTIONS
None. The hook takes no arguments and reads no environment variables; its
only input is the JSON event on standard input.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The call is allowed: another tool, or a
.B Bash
command with none of the forbidden characters and no
.B NAME=
prefix.
.TP
.B 2
The call is blocked: forbidden syntax, a command or argument outside the
argument policy, or a per\-beat limit reached. Standard error carries one
line beginning
.BR checkin\-bash\-guard: .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/bin/checkin\-bash\-guard
The hook.
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/checkin\-settings.json
Names the hook as the only one configured
.RB ( disableAllHooks
is false). A hook whose command does not exist fails open (Claude Code treats
a non-2 exit as a warning), so
.B run\-checkin
refuses to start (exit 1,
.BR "REFUSING to start" )
if the guard file is missing, if this file does not parse to
.B disableAllHooks
false with a Bash PreToolUse command hook at
.IR $FLEET_CODE/bin/checkin\-bash\-guard ,
or if piping a redirect payload through the guard does not exit 2 (a missing
python would otherwise fail open).
.SH EXAMPLES
A blocked call, as the orchestrator would see it in the beat's log:
.PP
.EX
checkin\-bash\-guard: shell syntax is not allowed on a beat (>). Run one
granted command with plain arguments; reword text that needs these characters.
.EE
.PP
Try the guard by hand:
.PP
.EX
echo '{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"ls | sh"}}' \e
  | /opt/cti\-agent/bin/checkin\-bash\-guard; echo $?
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR fleet\-timers (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
fleet\-kit/fleet/CLAUDE.md; SECURITY.md, "What the orchestrator may run".
